  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1023 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nHigh pressure will continue to slide westward into the Desert \nSouthwest through the end of the week. This will contribute to a \ngradual warming and slight drying trend, as the deeper plume of \nmonsoonal moisture is shifted to the east. That said, surface \nmoisture will linger under the high, with forecast PWAT values \nremaining 100-150% of normal through the end of the week. Limited \ndynamic forcing means that showers and thunderstorms will rely on \norographic lift to form, resulting activity firing off the higher \nterrain each afternoon through the end of the week. Frequent \nlightning and gusty winds will be possible from any storms that \nform, along with localized heavy rain. Should storms form over a \nsensitive area, or train over the same area, flash flooding will be \npossible, along with debris flows associated with recent burn scars. \nAs the high settles into the Southwest, temperatures will warm a \ndegree or two each day. By the end of the week, highs will be 5-10 \ndegrees above normal for late August.\n\nA pattern shift will be possible late this week into the coming \nweekend, as a deep trough approaches the West Coast. Ahead of this \ntrough, the tightening pressure gradient will lead to stronger \nwinds. There is potential for at least localized Critical Fire \nWeather conditions, but that all depends on whether drier air is \nable to push in, or if wetter conditions will prevail. Guidance is \nin poor agreement at this time, as some deterministic and ensemble \nsolutions favor wetter conditions, while others are running drier. \nIt's something to stay tuned for. Troughing gradually overspreads \nthe western CONUS through the weekend, introducing a wetter and \ncooler pattern. Current blended guidance is indicating temperatures \nnear to a few degrees below normal as we move into early September.",
